   "en": {
    "headline": "Nolan Lawson measures how much it costs an AI agent to build the same app with 12 JavaScript frameworks",
    "summary_short": "An experiment compares 12 JavaScript frameworks when an AI agent writes the code. The simpler ones come out cheaper and produce less code, countering the habit of choosing by gut feeling.",
    "summary_long": "Developer Nolan Lawson asked Claude Opus 5.5 to build the same Pokémon app five times with each of 12 JavaScript frameworks. He measured cost, time, tokens, lines of code and Lighthouse performance. His goal is to show that frameworks can be chosen based on evidence rather than gut feeling, even when agents do the coding.\n\nHono with htmx was the cheapest ($2.67 median per app) and Angular the most expensive ($4.97). SvelteKit, Gracile+Lit and Angular came out more expensive. Performance and accessibility scores were nearly perfect across the board, but the JavaScript shipped ranges from 0 KiB with Astro to 142 KiB with Next.js. Hono+htmx needed 1,370 lines versus Angular's 2,009, which matters because more code means more tokens to maintain it. The author warns that results vary a lot between runs and that the app is small and has little interactivity.",
    "unconfirmed_claims": []
   },
