   "en": {
    "headline": "Apollo's chief economist argues AI hasn't improved profits at large non-tech companies",
    "summary_short": "Apollo's Torsten Slok sees no gains in margins or productivity at large US companies outside tech. He doubts cheap AI will soon translate into higher profits.",
    "summary_long": "Torsten Slok, chief economist at Apollo Global Management, has analyzed what he calls the \"S&P 493\": the S&P 500 minus Alphabet, Amazon, Apple, Meta, Microsoft, Nvidia and Tesla. According to his note, profit margins and revenue per worker remain flat, with no sign that AI is improving productivity. For Slok, despite massive investment, AI has delivered \"zero\" financial payoff to non-tech corporate America.\n\nSlok doesn't think it's just a matter of time. He argues that AI may be \"a great calculator,\" but in business intelligence is mostly judgment. In his view, AI doesn't understand accountability, trust, culture or ethics, factors that ultimately drive revenue and margins. TechCrunch notes that other recent studies also find no significant productivity gains.",
    "unconfirmed_claims": []
